    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:ec46e3209ab74818",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "ad6d3815d423689a1d41d5cd6fa6cf963f27e799c41bc67c7f72013e6ec543d3",
      "text": "At the scheduled time, transfer from your hotel to the airport for your flight to Medellín. On arrival, meet your English-speaking guide and continue to El Peñón de Guatapé.\n\nThe rock stands between the towns of El Peñol and Guatapé. It was considered sacred by the Tahamí people. A local legend tells of a fish named Batolito that shielded people from the wrath of the gods and turned into a giant rock.\n\nThe rock is about 220 metres high, with a substantial portion below ground. It is estimated to be more than 70 million years old. El Peñón has been protected by the state since the 1940s. The first ascent, in 1954, took five days.\n\nThe elevated vantage point offers views of the waterways and green hills. You can try fruit beer near the entrance. After the visit, head to Guatapé, known for its brightly coloured façades with reliefs depicting scenes of local life. There will be time for a coffee and a stroll.\n\nReturn to Medellín, check in at your hotel and relax.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261109-986f3332f46d53ddb5737edd",
        "model": "Codex GPT-6"
      }
    },
